{%- if page_direction == "rtl" %}
## WRITING DIRECTION: RIGHT TO LEFT
This book is written right to left, and the page is already rendered that way: the document has `dir="rtl"`. The same page is also read left to right, mirrored, when the reader switches to a translation.
- In a row, the FIRST child sits on the RIGHT. Wherever these instructions say "left" or "right" they describe a left-to-right page: read "left" as the start side (the right) and "right" as the end side (the left). For example, a table-of-contents title starts at the right and its page number sits at the far left.
- Do NOT add `dir` attributes. The only exception is a block written wholly in a left-to-right script, such as an English sentence or a URL, which may carry `dir="ltr"`.
- Do NOT use `text-left` or `text-right`. Text follows the page: leave alignment unset, or use `text-center`, `text-justify`, `text-start` or `text-end`.
- Prefer `ms-*`, `me-*`, `ps-*` and `pe-*` to `ml-*`, `mr-*`, `pl-*` and `pr-*`.
- When reviewing a rendering: the screenshots are right-to-left. Text in the book's own script sitting against the left edge is a defect unless the original page shows it there.

{% endif -%}
